<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Pairing, both ends of it: handing the key to a new device over the LAN, authenticated by a code
     read off one screen and typed into the other.

     One layout for two roles, because they are the same conversation seen from opposite sides and
     share every part of it: a title, a line of explanation, something turning while waiting, and one
     button. The provider shows a code and waits; the joiner looks, picks, and types the code it is
     being shown. PairSheet turns the pieces on and off.

     The same surface as the peer sheet: a bottom sheet, which also means predictive back for free.
     24dp gutters, matching it, so the two read as one app. -->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:id="@+id/pair_root"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:orientation="vertical"
    android:paddingBottom="16dp">

    <com.google.android.material.bottomsheet.BottomSheetDragHandleView
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content" />

    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/pair_title"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceTitleLarge"
        android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurface" />

    <!-- The one line of explanation, rewritten at every state. minLines, and this is the reason it
         is here rather than left to wrap_content: this view carries eight different strings over the
         life of the sheet, the two bodies, "Found these...", "Enter the code shown on...", and four
         endings, and they are not the same number of lines. Left to itself the sheet grew and shrank
         by a line of body text every time one replaced another, which on the joining side is three
         times in the few seconds between picking a device and being paired. Two lines is what the
         longest of the ordinary ones takes on a narrow phone, so reserving two costs nothing in the
         states that need two and holds the sheet still in the ones that need one.

         minLines and not a fixed height: it is a reservation, not a cap. Anything longer still wraps,
         and the blank line a shorter string leaves behind is part of the same TextView, so TalkBack
         reads the string and nothing else; there is no empty view here to focus. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/pair_text"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:paddingTop="4dp"
        android:lineSpacingExtra="2dp"
        android:minLines="2"
        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodyMedium"
        android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant" />

    <!-- The code, on the device that holds the key: nine digits, shown as "123 456 789". The spaces
         are decoration and nothing else; Pairing.grouped puts them in for this view alone, and what
         is generated, compared and fed to the key derivation is the nine digits. Read aloud across a
         room and typed on a phone in the other hand, and a nine-digit run with nothing to hold onto
         is a nine-digit run said out loud wrong. Monospace so every character takes the same width
         and the eye can group them without the text reflowing as they change.

         THE WIDTH BUDGET, which the grouping spent all of. Monospace advance is one em per
         character, tracking adds to every glyph including the last, so the line is
         n × (1 + spacing) em at the text size in use. The one number here that was ever measured
         rather than derived is 10.35 em at textAppearanceDisplaySmall (36sp): nine digits at 0.15
         tracking, the width that just fit a narrow phone inside the 24dp gutters. Everything below
         is held under 10.35 × 36sp ≈ 373sp. The gutters are not negotiable.

           * grouped, n = 11 (nine digits + two spaces), tracking 0.25: 13.75 em, far over. The
             tracking is what has to go: two spaces separate the groups better than tracking ever
             separated the digits, so it buys nothing now and costs 11 x 0.25 = 2.75 em.
           * grouped, n = 11, tracking 0, at displaySmall (36sp): 11.0 em x 36 = 396sp. STILL 6%
             over the proven width. Tracking alone does not pay for the spaces.
           * grouped, n = 11, tracking 0, at headlineLarge (32sp): 11.0 em x 32 = 352sp, i.e. 5.5%
             INSIDE the 373sp already proven to fit. This is what is set below.

         One step down the type scale and no further, and no dp anywhere: a hardcoded size would be a
         guess that survives the next font-scale setting only by luck, whereas headlineLarge is still
         display type and still scales with the user's own text size, which 373sp was measured under.
         A narrower blank, U+2009 thin space instead of U+0020, was the other way to buy the 6%,
         and it is not taken: in a monospace font a space has the same one-em advance as a digit if
         the font has the glyph at all, and if it does not the line falls back to another font and
         the arithmetic above stops being arithmetic.

         This is a single-line TextView, so tracking changes its width and never its height. The size
         change does change the line's height, but this view is GONE for the whole of the joining
         side and never shares a state with the code field, so nothing there measures it. On the
         offering side the height is steady for the other reason below: the placeholder is the same
         eleven characters at the same size as the real code.

         It starts as nine greyed digits, grouped the same way, rather than empty, and that is what
         stops the sheet jumping open. The code cannot exist yet when this is laid out, because deriving it
         is a deliberately slow scrypt on a worker, so an empty view meant the sheet measured itself
         without a line of display type and then grew by exactly that much a moment later. Digits and
         not dashes because the two do not draw to the same height: a row of dashes reserved the
         width but not the line. -->
    <TextView
        android:id="@+id/pair_code"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:gravity="center"
        android:fontFamily="monospace"
        android:letterSpacing="0"
        android:text="@string/pair_code_placeholder"
        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceHeadlineLarge"
        android:textColor="?attr/colorPrimary"
        android:visibility="gone" />

    <!-- Waiting. Indeterminate is honest about a browse: nothing here knows how long a multicast
         answer takes, and a determinate bar would be inventing one.

         LoadingIndicator, which is M3 Expressive's own: a morphing sequence of shapes rather than
         the spinning arc of CircularProgressIndicator. The arc is the Material 2 shape that M3
         inherited, and next to Expressive's springy motion everywhere else on this surface it reads
         as a different app's idea of waiting. Left on its default style deliberately: its size and
         colours come from the theme, which is the whole point of using the component instead of
         drawing one.

         GONE when it is not wanted, like everything else here, rather than INVISIBLE: reserving a
         row of space for it through the whole of the code-entry screen would push the field down
         into a sheet that already reserves two lines of body text and a permanent error line,
         leaving a tall sheet with a squeezed field, worse than an edge that slides.
         Ui.visibilityMotion animates the height change, so the row growing in is a slide and not a
         jump. -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/pair_progress"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:paddingHorizontal="24dp"
        android:gravity="center_vertical"
        android:orientation="horizontal"
        android:visibility="gone">

        <com.google.android.material.loadingindicator.LoadingIndicator
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content" />

        <!-- 0dp + weight, not match_parent. In a horizontal LinearLayout a match_parent child is
             measured against the WHOLE parent width rather than what is left of it, so this row
             measured wider than the sheet and squeezed everything sharing a line with it. Weight is
             how "the rest of the row" is spelled here. -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/pair_progress_text"
            android:layout_width="0dp"
            android:layout_weight="1"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_marginStart="16dp"
            android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodyMedium"
            android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant" />
    </LinearLayout>

    <!-- Devices found, one card each, filled in by PairSheet. -->
    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/pair_list"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="12dp"
        android:orientation="vertical"
        android:visibility="gone" />

    <!-- errorEnabled up front, which is the opposite of what Ui.showError does elsewhere in this app
         and is the point: there the error line is collapsed when the complaint goes, because a
         settings form has a dozen fields and a dozen permanent gaps. Here there is one field, and
         everything it can be told, such as "Nine digits", or what the connection failed with, arrives
         while the user is staring at the sheet waiting for a verdict. Enabling the line from the
         start reserves it once, so a wrong code adds a message instead of adding a message AND a row
         of height. The reserved line is empty text in a view with no content, so it is not announced
         and cannot be focused.

         PairSheet therefore calls setError directly and never Ui.showError: that helper's
         setErrorEnabled(false) would undo this on the first cleared error. -->
    <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout
        android:id="@+id/pair_code_layout"
        style="?attr/textInputOutlinedStyle"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:layout_marginHorizontal="24dp"
        android:hint="@string/pair_code_hint"
        app:errorEnabled="true"
        android:visibility="gone">

        <!-- number, not numberPassword or text: the code is not a secret from the person holding the
             phone, and a digits-only keyboard is the whole point. It is also what makes a typed
             space harmless: inputType="number" installs a digits-only filter, which drops spaces
             from typing and from a paste alike, but that is a convenience and NOT the guarantee.
             PairSheet.connect strips every non-digit before it measures or derives anything, because
             "most IMEs filter it" is not a thing to derive a key from.

             maxLength is 11 and not Pairing.CODE_DIGITS, and the difference is the grouping: the
             other device SHOWS "123 456 789", so that is what gets copied down and typed, and nine
             digits plus two spaces is eleven characters. A cap of nine would silently swallow the
             last two digits of a code the user typed exactly as they were shown, the worst of the
             possible failures, because what is left looks complete. Eleven is a bound on the field,
             not a definition of the code: PairSheet strips the spaces and then requires exactly
             Pairing.CODE_DIGITS digits, so eleven digits is refused like anything else that is not
             nine. -->
        <com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputEditText
            android:id="@+id/pair_code_input"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:inputType="number"
            android:maxLength="11"
            android:imeOptions="actionDone"
            android:fontFamily="monospace"
            android:letterSpacing="0.2" />
    </com.google.android.material.textfield.TextInputLayout>

    <!-- The one button, GONE while a code is being checked: there is nothing to press then, and the
         progress row above takes its place (see pair_progress for why neither reserves space for
         the other). -->
    <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
        android:id="@+id/pair_action"
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_gravity="end"
        android:layout_marginTop="16dp"
        android:layout_marginHorizontal="24dp"
        android:visibility="gone" />
</LinearLayout>
